WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.820
Let's talk about music.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.240
"Despacito ft.Daddy Yankee by Luis Fonsi plays"

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.340
No no no, not that stuff. I'm talking about music in anime.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.620
The good shit.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.960
"Dreaming Traveler of an Integrated Circuit by Hiro Shimono plays"

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.600
With any medium music can be used as a powerful weapon,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.160
and anime is no exception.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.660
As all through its history there have been some absolutely beautiful examples littered throughout.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.940
With this video I wanted to show a little appreciation

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.020
about some of the ways music has been used in anime.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:34.320
And for anyone who got confused with my animation video,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.620
this is not meant to be an in-depth breakdown. This is not a deep analysis.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.420
This is just my casual music appreciation thread.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.040
So to clear things up, this time let's just name the video

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.700
Let's just call this "Music In Anime".

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.580
To make things easier to digest this time,

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.340
I've separated everything I want to talk about

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.120
to some nice little bite-sized categories.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.960
So let's start things off with

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:05.500
By having a unique quality to the soundtrack or even a single song,

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.500
perhaps using a genre or instrument you wouldn't normally associate with that type of show

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.140
allows the score to stand out even more, and if done right

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.560
enhances the experience or even revolutionizes

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:18.330
the perception we have between a type of music and a genre of media.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.980
I'm talking about the use of bagpipes in Fairy Tail,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.620
how Nujabes' beats in Samurai Champloo (sponsored by Herbal Essences)

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.580
completely transformed the association we have of lo-fi hip-hop beats to traditional samurai,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.140
with Eurobeat and Initial D doing a very similar thing to street racing.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.400
And using free jazz in Gundam Thunderbolts,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.880
perfectly blending the controlled chaos of jazz with the actual chaos of a battlefield.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.880
Strengthened by its innovative use of mixing the diegetic jazz music our characters are listening to

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.660
with the non-diegetic score.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.580
Wohoo, yeah look at those big boy words.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.610
A unique score can add so much personality to a show giving it that extra layer of appeal.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.880
And I think the best example I can use to hammer this point home

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.180
is when comparing the original Hellsing anime to Hellsing Ultimate.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.720
Now anyone who's watched both series can pretty much agree

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.620
that Hellsing Ultimate is better in basically every way,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.540
except for arguably the music.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.760
Now this isn't to say the music was bad, far from it.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.940
If we look at the soundtrack that's mainly comprised of orchestral tracks with the occasional opera choir,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.000
it's very suiting for the themes and tone of the show, but that's kind of it.

00:02:18.239 --> 00:02:22.018
It does its job and does it well, but there's nothing really distinct or unique about it.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.760
And that's saying something for a show about modern undead vampires fighting nazi zombies.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.580
Now if we listen to some of the tracks from the original (Hellsing anime)

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:38.600
(Alucard's Theme from Hellsing OST plays)

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.440
You hear it and you just think

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.600
"Oooh man. What is this sexy piano? Is it getting hot in here? Am I being seduced?"

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.060
"Damn, Alucard you smooth motherfucker."

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.540
"Butter me up and take me home because I'm sure you're about to show me a good time."

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.380
Alucard: "Nice.."

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:56.140
The jazzy influence beats of the original anime's OST just has so much more character and style in it,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:59.760
than the epic orchestral choir even if the latter is more thematically suited.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.240
And for that reason It's just far more memorable.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.420
When I think of Helsing my mind immediately goes back to these tracks,

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.140
even if I only remember the events of Hellsing Ultimate.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.700
Now I'm not saying the music always needs to stand out,

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.720
sometimes its job is to not detract from what's happening on screen.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.549
I'm just highlighting what it could possibly add to it.

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:21.580
But there are some anime where the music is definitely one of, if not the main stars of the show.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.860
Here I'm talking about anime where the music is such an essential part of the show's identity,

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.600
that to replace the music would be to essentially change the anime at its core.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.000
I'm talking about things like Cowboy Bebop's legendary musical numbers,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.980
Radwimps' contribution to Your Name

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:41.480
and Daft Punk's full-on animated album in Interstella 5555

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.060
5, 5, 5, 5

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.620
Hi, is this Domino's Pizza?

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.560
I think a lot of these can be attributed to the fact that in these examples

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.140
there were scenes in the anime that were tweaked according to the music,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.700
as opposed to the music being composed after the visuals had been done.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.420
(Makoto) Shinkai changed up scenes in the scripts according to the songs Radwimps composed,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:03.460
with very similar things happening in how (Yoko) Kanno and (Shinichiro) Watanabe were working during (Cowboy) Bebop,

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.040
and Interstella five-six-seven-eight was a music video stretched over an album.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.800
So yeah, we could play You Say Run over it, but why would we?

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.800
Stupid question, let's do it anyway.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.020
(You Say Run from My Hero Academia OST plays)

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.400
As I mentioned in my Your Name video though,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.520
the series that epitomizes this the most is FLCL's iconic rock soundtrack done by The Pillows.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.460
Now I've already said that the entire show feels like a string of loosely connected music videos.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.860
I mean, fuck they certainly weren't connected by plot,

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:35.360
He says watching a red robot pop out of a literal dickhead to suplex a bigger robot hand

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.640
before being domesticated by getting his head smashed in with a bass guitar.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.320
God, I miss this fucking show!

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.900
Which is why one of the few things that holds the series together

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.919
and gives it a bit of cohesion is The Pillows' music.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.360
The songs don't feel like they are tracks written to accompany any kind of visuals,

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.860
it feels like just a full-on album as even the tracks without vocals are instrumentals of tracks with them.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.940
But because of this and because of the way the scenes were integrated with the songs,

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.120
these songs and the band are a central part of the show.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.040
It's to the point where all my pessimism and skepticism surrounding the newly announced sequels

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.280
culminated in this reaction for the trailer.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.180
Okay, this is looking all right. I mean, I'm still not too excited

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.560
cause we don't really need a sequel, and I'm still highly wary cause none of the original team are behind-

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.740
This thing is gonna be amazing! It's gonna be the best thing ever! Nothing could possibly go wrong! WHOOO!

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.540
As with entire soundtracks that we associate with the show,

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.620
singular tracks can also carry the same if not a much heavier weight behind it.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.240
And I'd like to talk about that in this section called

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:34.680
Heh, I know what you're thinking.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.480
"Gigguk, you literary title genius."

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:38.040
I know.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.580
We can look at how music can be used to condition you into feeling an emotional response.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:42.860
Let me give you an example.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:46.260
For anyone who's seen Anohana. What do you feel when you hear this track?

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:53.280
(Secret Base from Anohana OST plays)

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.900
Uhh, be right back, gonna go cry myself to sleep again!

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.640
Now I actually love Anohana but even I have wised up

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.680
at the use of this song at the end of every episode which plays at the most emotional moments.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.340
And because of this, every time we hear the track we associate it with feeling sad and emotional

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.740
which builds and builds culminating in the final episodes which I can only describe as a

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.860
full-on bukakke of ugly cry.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:18.640
It's like with Pavlov's Dog, the experiment in the 1890s where Ivan Pavlov

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.200
managed to condition a dog into salivating by ringing a bell every time the dog was given food,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.480
to the point where the dog would start salivating at the bell sound even when there was no food.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.280
Ivan Pavlov was probably there looking at his dog like "Heh, what a stupid dog."

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:34.280
"What a stupid dog to be tricked by such a simple trick."

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:38.460
And here humanity is a 100 years later going "Uhuhu Menma!"

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.640
And you see this in use all over anime, Clannad, Little Busters, hell pretty much every Key show.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.040
And it's not just limited to sad responses either.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:46.540
You have

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:48.480
How about the...

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:51.680
And...

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.140
You know, the character that's hired a guy to follow them around with the boombox everywhere

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.400
so every time they appear on screen the music shouts

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.200
"HEY GUYS, IT'S YA BOI COMING AT YOU LIVE!"

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.680
"BE SURE TO SMASH THAT LIKE BUTTON TO SEE ME ANNIHILATE KIRA."

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.560
By building association you build the impact it can have on the audience.

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.460
You can even boil it down to a melody, for example,

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.380
you can have a simple baby version where you lay out a melody so the audience builds some familiarization.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.600
Then when you really needed it to hit you, inject the testosterone,

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:24.040
increase the musical layers, and you just let it rip for full effect.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.360
Conversely it can also be used to subvert your expectations.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:40.040
Bleach had one of the most unique victory songs in Shonen.

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.320
I mean speaking of hiring a boom box carrier, Ichigo hide a full-on group of soul singers

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.960
to follow him about every time he was about to do something awesome.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:52.080
But one effective way they were able to frame how completely overpowered Aizen was as a villain

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.500
was to have him do this.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:00.840
Did you catch that?

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:07.240
No, it's not the fact that he caught the sword with one finger. It's that he literally stops the bloody victory music!

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:08.260
Fucking what?!

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.800
By completely subverting the association of that track with Ichigo kicking ass,

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:16.220
it told us that there was absolutely no safe space with this villain which was a fantastic way

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.060
to build up how much of a threats this individual was.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.220
Wooh, that was a long segment. What are we talking about next?

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:27.400
(Sorairo Days from Gurren Lagann OST plays)

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.740
Oh, yeah. How could I forget,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:34.530
...or as I like to call it...

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.800
You know the drill. We've reached the end. It's episode 24.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:43.640
Episode 12, for all you hip millennials out there.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.780
We're in extra time, bases are loaded and all hope looks lost.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:52.100
But as your main boy gears up for one final attack you hear the first few beats of a familiar tune.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.320
It's the opening.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.500
You look over to your pal Tommy and he's looking at you.

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:00.740
Both of you aren't prepared, but you both know what has to happen.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:04.480
You give a final approving nod, and as the chills run down your spine,

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:11.140
in sync you both unzip your pants because you know, IT'S TIME, TO NUT.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.360
What I described was probably a 100% accurate description

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.380
of an experience you've had in a climax of an anime.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:23.640
And this goes back to the music that we associate with stuff.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.420
As you have to listen to an opening every time you watch a show,

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:29.440
or you know at least once if you end up skipping most openings like me.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:30.560
What this means is-

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.980
Wait, what?! "If you end up skipping most openings like me." "If you end up skipping most openings like me."

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.080
"If you end up skipping most openings like me."

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.560
"911, what's your emergency?"

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:45.720
"Hi, I need to... I like to report a hate crime please."

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.800
What this means is there is that guaranteed association we talked about earlier.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.700
But because most openings are designed to hype you up to see the actual show,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.540
that is the perfect emotional association to have

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.240
which is why playing the opening at the show's climax is pretty much a guaranteed insta-cum

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:03.380
even though everyone uses this technique.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.800
Lastly, I wanted to quickly mention

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:11.580
Great music can elevates a good anime into all new heights.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.220
But what I finds way more interesting is for me,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.000
how it can make bad anime kind of watchable and enjoyable.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.720
I remember watching the first few seconds of Guilty Crown and being

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:24.080
immediately captivated by the opening scene playing this beautiful song.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.560
What a way to open a show.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.659
You look at this opening and you think "Man, this is gonna be something special."

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:43.120
"This is something I need to pay attention to because it could turn out fantastic."

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.660
And then the second half of the series hits, and it's just like..

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:48.700
HAHAHAHAHHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHA

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:49.640
YOU FOOL!

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:52.980
But what gets me is that despite the complete mess that the show turned into,

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:57.260
there were still scenes that grabs me when it had absolutely no right to do so.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:01.460
Because it's hard not to be just a little bit engrossed for a second when you have beautiful tracks

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:04.560
alongside the absolutely bombastic action music.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.360
even if the scenes that accompanied them were a fucking mess of a dog turd.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.640
See, that's the thing about good music pieces.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.460
It can make you, for a second, forgets about the rest of the shitshow surrounding a series

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:19.280
to help engross you into a scene or a moment because that's the power it has.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.200
As much as I have memed this scene from Tokyo Ghoul √A,

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:26.300
even I have to admit that the acoustic version of Unravel is powerful as fuck.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.340
Never before has the voice of a man who sounds like

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:31.760
he permanently has his bollocks trapped in a vice grip sounded so soothing.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:37.020
In fact the entire soundtrack of Tokyo Ghouls second season, out of nowhere was pretty damn awesome

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.580
compared to the previous season, despite the season itself being...

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:41.820
Uhhh

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:43.500
HAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHA

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:48.220
Because you know what, taken by itself I actually really really fucking liked this scene.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.380
The raw musical piece paired with the slow pacing of Kaneki

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.800
and the long shots made for this soothing experience.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.800
But taken in the context of the rest of the series, and it's just like..

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:03.020
"Boulevard Of Broken Dreams by Green Day plays."

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:04.000
Uhhh

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.960
Okay, what about the rest of the characters?

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:09.500
Is, is Tokyo (Ghoul) still running?

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.880
What I want to end on is just talking about Zankyou No Terror for a bit,

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:18.500
because to this day it remains one of the most unique scores I've heard in an anime and one of my favorites.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:24.180
Not only taking inspiration from beautiful atmospheric Icelandic music which in itself is rare in anime,

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.930
but juxtaposing that against the hyper realistic art style in a chaotic plot about terrorism

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:33.180
which really shouldn't have worked, culminated in some of the most beautiful and poetic

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:37.060
experiences I've had watching anime even through its suspect writing.

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:40.820
It's what cemented Yoko Kanno as my favorite anime composer of all time.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:45.000
Which is a very cliche answer in the mountains of talented anime composers out there.

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:47.920
But what this showed me is how diverse she can be,

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.060
and after many many years of outstanding work in her years of working in anime,

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:55.820
was still able to pull out something so entirely different from anything we've heard before

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:58.780
yet still have it be an absolute masterpiece.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:02.680
In the end what I really wanted to hammer down is that music is something I highly value.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.100
Not only in anime but in my own work as well.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:07.460
One of my favorite compliments I can get from a comment is..

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:09.459
"Hey, I love the music choices for this video!"

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.340
And that's because I'm a dumb fucker who spends way too much time

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:16.020
trying to pick the perfect song choices to match the exact tone I have in my head

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.900
as I apply a lot of the things I've talked about in my own work.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:21.780
And I didn't even get to talk about everything I wanted to talk about.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:24.980
Music isn't always at the forefront of a show,

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:29.640
Sometimes it's simply the scores job to support the visuals, character and plot, which take the spotlight.

00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:33.369
But I have a soft spot for composition that go that extra mile.

00:13:33.369 --> 00:13:39.540
So, I hope in the least that this video has reminded you about some of your favorite uses of music in anime.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.620
Because, it's thanks to this magical world that I can proudly shove my phone playlist

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.780
on the speakers at a party to have people turn around and ask..

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:50.460
"Wow, where does this beautiful music come from?"

00:13:50.460 --> 00:14:01.560
(Dreaming Traveler of an Integrated Circuit by Hiro Shimono plays again)

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.800
("Ride on a Shooting Star" by The Pillows plays, under the following)

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:07.820
Hey guys, hope you enjoyed that video

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.830
thank you very much this month to -

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:18.800
and everyone else on Patreon for helping to support me for this month and making this video possible.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.760
This probably won't be the last time I make a video with focus on music in anime,

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:27.020
but these are just all the small things I wanted to mention that I feel didn't warrant their own video.

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.660
Anyway, I'm going to go back in my cave now till the end of year review,

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:34.139
so until then I've been Gigguk and I will see you all next time.

